    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, but can you get a bit more specific on the science? like what's actually happening with the central nervous system or the muscle fibers when they're stuck? i want to be able to explain the physiological reason to them so they don't think they're just losing progress.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When an athlete is stuck, the central nervous system (CNS) might not be sufficiently activated to recruit the maximum number of muscle fibers for a given lift, which is necessary for progress. Deloading gives the CNS a well-deserved break by relieving some of the constant stimulation, affording it a chance to recuperate and effectively signal the muscles when it’s time for a stronger lift. Furthermore, muscle fibers, particularly fast-twitch fibers responsible for strength and power, may also become inhibited. This inhibition can happen due to the accumulation of metabolic byproducts, which affect the muscles' ability to contract effectively. By temporarily reducing the workload and volume, the body can clear out these byproducts and, encouraging recovery, muscle fibers to produce their full stimulation capacity, potentially leading to increased strength. Additionally, new motor learning can be incorporated as the athlete returns to training with a clearer mind and possibly slightly altered technique, setting the stage for progressing past the plateau.",
      "source": "target",
